{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Among whom I dwell.</b> Abraham disclosed his reasoning for why it was necessary to make him swear — and why this particular argument would not work, namely that they are blessed and he is cursed. For the servant entertained the thought that even though Canaan is cursed, he had already emerged from the curse, for the verse said ״וֶהְיֵה בְּרָכָה״ (Genesis 12:2) — meaning that any family among whom Abraham dwelt would be blessed. The verse also says ״וְנִבְרְכוּ בְךָ״ (Genesis 12:3) and so forth. Therefore he said <b>among whom I</b> dwell — meaning: even though I dwell among them and they are blessed through me, nevertheless this does not remove the curse from his cursedness. He also intended to forestall the claim that, since he lived peaceably among them and they did him no harm as neighbors, he should intermarry with them. Therefore he said ״among whom I dwell״ — even so, this argument will not avail to intermarry with them."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:57.979812066Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}